					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/circlesandsquares" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1367458); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1367458&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607633444" alt="CirclesAndSquares" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1367458)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/circlesandsquares" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1367458); return false;" >CirclesAndSquares</a></span> <span class="odate time_1412654067 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Oct 2014 03:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2126091">
									

<p>I feel like the interviews are basically exposition. Phrases like &quot;Now that I think of it, we have a strange society.&quot; don't feel genuine to me. If I were suddenly taken out of my element, I don't think I'd have such an objective opinions of my world. I think this would be more successful if we were able to see the cars' world not because it is explained to us, but because they are so a part of it that their actions and speech paint a picture for us. &quot;I hate my peers. I hate this society.&quot; Those phrases ring false to me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
